As we know, $K_{p} = K_{c}$ × (RT)^{$\Delta n_{g}$}

So, for reaction having same number of gaseous moles on reactants and products side will have same value of Kc and Kp otherwise their values are different.

For reaction, $2NO_{(g)} \rightleftharpoons N_{2(g)} + O_{2(g)} \Delta n_{g}$ = 2 – 2 = 0

$\Rightarrow K_{p} = K_{c}$ × (RT)^{0}

$\therefore K_{p} = K_{c}$

For reaction, $SO_{2(g)} + NO_{2(g)} \rightleftharpoons SO_{3(g)} + NO_{(g)} \Delta n_{g}$ = 2 – 2 = 0

$\Rightarrow K_{p} = K_{c}$ × (RT)^{0}

$\therefore K_{p} = K_{c}$

For reaction, $H_{2(g)} + I_{2(g)} \rightleftharpoons 2HI_{(g)} \Delta n_{g}$ = 2 – 2 = 0

$\Rightarrow K_{p} = K_{c}$ × (RT)^{0}

$\therefore K_{p} = K_{c}$

For reaction, $H_{2(g)} + I_{2(g)} \rightleftharpoons 2HI_{(g)} \Delta n_{g}$ = 2 – 2 = 0

$\Rightarrow K_{p} = K_{c}$ × (RT)^{0}

$\therefore K_{p} = K_{c}$

For reaction, $2C_{(s)} + O_{2(g)} \rightleftharpoons 2CO_{2(g)} \Delta n_{g}$ = 2 – 3 = -1

$\Rightarrow K_{p} = K_{c}$ × (RT)^{-1}

$\therefore K_{p} \ne K_{c}$

Rate = k[A]^{x} [B]^{y}

For the given situations

(I) rate = k(0.1)^{x} (0.1)^{y} = 6.0$\times$10^{ - 3}

(II) rate = k(0.2)^{x} (0.3)^{y} = 7.2$\times$10^{ - 2}

(III) rate = k(0.3)^{x} (0.4)^{y} = 2.88$\times$10^{ - 1}

(IV) rate = k(0.4)^{x} (0.1)^{y} = 2.40$\times$10^{ - 2}

Dividing eq. (I) by eq. (IV) we get

${$\left( {{{0.1} $\over$ {0.4}}} \right)$^x}{$\left( {{{0.1} $\over$ {0.1}}} \right)$^y} = {{6.0 \times {{10}^{ - 3}}} \over {2.4 \times {{10}^{ - 2}}}} \Rightarrow {$\left( {$\frac{1}{4}$} \right)$^x} = {$\left( {$\frac{1}{4}$} \right)$^1} \Rightarrow$ x = 1

On dividing eq. (II) by eq. (III) we get

${$\left( {{{0.3} $\over$ {0.3}}} \right)$^x}{$\left( {{{0.2} $\over$ {0.4}}} \right)$^y} = {{7.2 \times {{10}^{ - 2}}} \over {2.88 \times {{10}^{ - 1}}}} \Rightarrow {$\left( {$\frac{1}{2}$} \right)$^y} = \frac{1}{4} \Rightarrow$ y = 2

$\therefore$  Rate = k[A][B]^{2}
